!Sample_type = SRA
!Sample_channel_count = 1
!Sample_source_name_ch1 = Merkel cell carcinoma cell line
!Sample_organism_ch1 = Homo sapiens
!Sample_taxid_ch1 = 9606
!Sample_characteristics_ch1 = cell line: MKL-1
!Sample_characteristics_ch1 = cell type: Merkel cell carcinoma
!Sample_characteristics_ch1 = merkel cell polyomavirus: positive
!Sample_characteristics_ch1 = chip antibody: none
!Sample_growth_protocol_ch1 = MKL-1 cells grow as suspension clumps in RPMI medium supplemented with 10% FBS, Penicillin-Streptomycin, and glutamax
!Sample_molecule_ch1 = genomic DNA
!Sample_extract_protocol_ch1 = Lysates were clarified from sonicated nuclei as described in 'ChIP-seq: using high-throughput sequencing to discover protein-DNA interactions. 2009 Methods 48, no. 3: 240-248.'
!Sample_extract_protocol_ch1 = 10 ng of DNA from ChIP experiments or input DNA were prepared for sequencing with NEBNext ChIP-seq Library Prep Reagent Set for Illumina (New England Biolabs). Diluted libraries were used for 50 cycles single-end sequencing on HiSeq2000 system (Illumina) at the Center for Cancer Computational Biology (CCCB) at Dana-Farber Cancer Institute following the manufacturer’s protocol.
!Sample_data_processing = FASTQ data were mapped to human hg19 genome using Bowtie (bowtie-0.12.7)
!Sample_data_processing = Duplicate peaks were removed (macs2 filterdup function) and the reads were normalized (macs2 randsample) so that the input and the ChIP sample have the same number of reads using MACS2 (MACS-2.0.10.20130306).
!Sample_data_processing = Peaks were called using MACS2 with hs as the genome size (macs2 callpeak ).
!Sample_data_processing = Using BETA(1.0.5), the ChIP-seq peaks were incorporated with the differential expression data (diffexp.txt file from RNA-seq analysis) to find the direct targets and predict motifs.   Parameters: BETA plus --peakfile STHA_1.peaks_peaks.bed --diff_expr diffexp.txt --kind O --genome hg19
!Sample_data_processing = genome build: hg19
